Here is your **Phase 1 Implementation Plan: Core Scaffolding**.

In this phase, we are establishing the foundational architecture. We will intentionally skip the OS-level sandboxing and tools for now. By the end of this phase, you will have a Next.js application hooked up to a local Ollama model, orchestrated by a highly extensible **LangGraph.js state machine** with built-in thread memory, and a modern streaming chat UI.

Building the graph architecture *now* guarantees that when we inject the Linux tools in Phase 2, the routing logic and state management are already perfectly in place.

---

### Step 1: Ubuntu Infrastructure & Local Inference Setup

Before writing any Next.js code, we need to stand up the local inference engine on your Ubuntu machine. We will use `Qwen-2.5-Coder` (or `Llama-3.1`), as they are the current state-of-the-art open weights for reliable tool-calling and reasoning.

Open your Ubuntu terminal:

```bash
# 1. Install Ollama on your host OS
curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh

# 2. Ensure the Ollama service is running
sudo systemctl enable ollama
sudo systemctl start ollama

# 3. Pull the primary reasoning model
# Recommendation: qwen2.5-coder:7b (excellent for programming and terminal tasks)
ollama pull qwen2.5-coder:7b

```

